<article class="env-lemma" id="0H7D">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0H7D">Lemma <span data-tag="0H7D">29.55.9</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a scheme with locally finitely many irreducible components. The normalization morphism $\nu : X^\nu \to X$ is an isomorphism if and only if $X$ is normal. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $\nu $ is an isomorphism, then $X$ is normal by Lemma <a href="/tag/035Q" data-tag="035Q">29.55.5</a>. Conversely, suppose $X$ is normal. By Lemma <a href="/tag/035K" data-tag="035K">29.54.6</a> and Properties, Lemma <a href="/tag/0357" data-tag="0357">28.7.5</a> we may assume $X$ is integral. By Lemma <a href="/tag/035Q" data-tag="035Q">29.55.5</a> the morphism $\nu $ is integral and $X^\nu $ has a unique irreducible component (so it is an integral scheme). We conclude from Lemmas <a href="/tag/0BXC" data-tag="0BXC">29.55.7</a> and <a href="/tag/0AB1" data-tag="0AB1">29.55.8</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>